# =============================================================================
# .gitignore - files that must NEVER be committed to version control
# =============================================================================

# --- Terraform ---------------------------------------------------------------
# State files record everything you built AND can contain sensitive values.
# Losing them means Terraform forgets your infrastructure exists (while it
# keeps billing). Leaking them can expose secrets. On a real team these live
# in an S3 bucket with DynamoDB locking, never in Git.
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
.terraform/
.terraform.lock.hcl
crash.log

# Your real settings, which often contain account-specific details.
# The committed terraform.tfvars.example is the safe template.
terraform.tfvars
*.auto.tfvars

# Saved plan files can contain sensitive variable values.
*.tfplan

# --- Our CLI scripts ---------------------------------------------------------
# The hand-rolled state file holding real AWS resource IDs.
.state.env
.state.env.tmp

# --- Credentials - the ones that actually hurt -------------------------------
# If an AWS access key reaches a public repo, bots find it within minutes.
*.pem
*.ppk
credentials
.env
.aws/

# --- Editors and operating systems -------------------------------------------
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.vscode/
.idea/
*.swp
*~
